Abstract = {Urban parks provide important ecological and social benefits but
   increasing urbanization and associated activities have led to the
   accumulation of potentially toxic elements in their soils, posing
   environmental and health risks. This study assessed soil pollution and
   associated ecological and human health risks in four urban parks (Letn
   \& aacute;, Riegrovy sady, Kampa, and Stromovka) in Prague, Czechia.
   Soil concentrations of Fe, Cu, Cr, Mg, Mn, Ni, Pb, and Zn were
   determined using X-ray fluorescence spectroscopy, and risks were
   evaluated using ecological indices and human health models for children
   and adults. Results showed that sandy loam soil was the dominant
   texture, while soil pH varied significantly, ranging from strongly
   acidic in Riegrovy (5.23) to near neutral in Letn \& aacute; (7.18).
   Potentially toxic element analysis revealed significant soil
   contamination, with the highest Cu (360 mg kg-1) and Zn (793 mg kg-1)
   concentrations in Riegrovy sady, while Kampa exhibited the highest
   levels of Pb (442 mg kg-1) and Mn (3225 mg kg-1). The ecological risk
   assessment identified Riegrovy sady
